    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d472">

                <pb facs="0671" n="657" xml:id="pg657"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">123 Service, John S.: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-24T08:00:00+07:00">October 24, 1944—8
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 11:53 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1722. Secretary John Service arrived here last evening from Yenan under
                    instructions [from] Stilwell’s Headquarters to proceed to Washington for
                    temporary duty War Department in connection military affairs this theater. He
                    leaves this morning for Washington under number one priority. Secretaries Davies
                    and Emmerson<anchor n="42" xml:id="d472-f1"/> left here Sunday morning for Yenan
                    under orders Stilwell. These movements made without prior consultation with or
                    notification to Embassy but officers named reported to me in person before
                    departure. Service not informed of reason for his transfer to Washington.</p>
                <p>Embassy has reason to believe that some of our Army officers and perhaps Stilwell
                    favor direct aid to Chinese Communist forces and that object in having Service
                    proceed to Washington has to do with some such proposal. Stilwell called on me
                    last Saturday before his departure for Washington but did not mention the
                    subject. While it still remains an official secret, it seems to be quite well
                    known here in Chinese circles that Stilwell is being replaced in this theater
                    upon insistence of President Chiang.<anchor n="43" xml:id="d472-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d472-f1" n="42" xml:id="d472-f1-content">
                <p>John Paton Davies, Jr., and John K. Emmerson, Second Secretaries of Embassy in
                    China, on detail to General Stilwell’s Headquarters.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d472-f2" n="43" xml:id="d472-f2-content">
                <p>See letter from the Ambassador in China, September 28, <ref target="#pg256">p.
                        256</ref>.</p>
            </note>
